  "image_name": "3fca900bbb0494780411d7d9bd874036.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares several electrochemical cell layouts for removing salt, showing how cations and anions move in different channel and membrane configurations. Across all designs, the blue gradient marks salt concentration in the flow channel, and arrows indicate ion transport toward uptake or release electrodes.\n\nThe top row shows one-channel systems. In the membrane-free version, both positive and negative ions leave the same central stream toward opposite electrodes. In the membrane-based version, ion-selective membranes are added on each side so cations pass through a CEM and anions through an AEM before reaching the electrodes.\n\nThe middle row introduces two-channel designs, where one stream is desalinated while a neighboring stream becomes more concentrated. Depending on whether the system is cation-based or anion-based, one ion crosses a selective membrane into the adjacent channel while the counter-ion is taken up or released at the electrodes.\n\nThe bottom row shows three-channel concepts. One uses multiple electrolytes flanking the saline channel, separated by both CEM and AEM, while the other replaces fixed electrodes with flow electrodes on both sides. Overall, the figure highlights how adding membranes and extra channels improves ion selectivity and separates salt removal from electrode reactions.",
